======================================================================================================================
DRIFT RECURRENCE -- climatology only, 3 historical cuts, target source 30m
======================================================================================================================
  60,000 episodes, 746 episode days, 2024-09-18 to 2026-10-04
  POPULATION: ALL only. Trending is a refinement and is not needed to
  answer a recurrence question.
  MODELS: Climatology only -- one unconditional empirical quantile vector
  per (cut, direction, horizon), fitted on EVERYTHING before the cut.
  Nothing from the halves analysis is refitted and no new model type exists.

  NOTE ON THE FIT RULE. The halves analysis fitted climatology on the TRAIN
  block only -- the first 80% of fit days -- so that it had exactly the data
  the conditional models had. There are no conditional models here, so there
  is nothing to match and the brief's rule applies: everything before the
  cut. That gives this track MORE fit data per cut than that baseline had,
  which if anything biases it AGAINST finding drift.

  30-MINUTE CLOSES. The true envelope is strictly worse; the worst point
  inside a bar is never observed. The bias is the same at every cut, so a
  comparison ACROSS cuts is unaffected -- which is all this script does.

======================================================================================================================
CUT POINTS -- derived from the constraints, not chosen
======================================================================================================================
  earliest admissible cut  2025-05-27   (episode day #251: exactly 250 precede it)
  latest   admissible cut  2026-04-29   (80 episode days from here to 2026-07-18)
  window 337 days, spacing 150 days -> 3 cuts.
  4 cuts at this spacing would need 3 gaps of 150 = 450 days of window against 337 available,
  landing the last at 2026-08-20, 113 days past the latest admissible cut.
  4 cuts DO fit at 112-day spacing; 113 misses by 2 day(s). Neither is 'roughly 150 days apart'.

  cut          train days train rows               near half                far half   near/far rows   q05 SE pp
  2025-05-27          250      5,391  2025-05-27..2025-07-05  2025-07-06..2025-08-14     1,138/1,569   0.65/0.55
  2025-10-24          400     11,238  2025-10-24..2025-12-02  2025-12-03..2026-01-11     2,906/3,260   0.40/0.38
  2026-03-23          550     24,382  2026-03-23..2026-05-01  2026-05-02..2026-06-10     4,610/6,342   0.32/0.27
  q05 SE is the binomial floor on the noise in a single coverage number.
  Day clustering widens the real interval. The effect being chased is
  1.99pp, so read the earliest cut's column against that.

  PROTECTED BLOCK AUDIT: rows scored here with day >= 2026-07-18: 0  (must be 0)
  CUT OVERLAP AUDIT: rows shared between any two cuts' test windows: 0  (must be 0 at 150-day spacing with 80-day windows)

  TRAIN/TEST OUTCOME OVERLAP, measured. An episode entered in the last hours
  before a cut has a 10.5-hour outcome window that reaches past it, so its
  target is partly shaped by near-half conditions. That contaminates the
  climatology estimate TOWARD the near half, which would shrink the near gap
  and inflate far-minus-near -- the exact direction of the hypothesis. So it
  is counted rather than reasoned away. Being unconditional does not make a
  sample estimate immune; it is the SIZE below that makes it immaterial.
    2025-05-27       5 of   5,391 train rows cross the cut    0.093%
    2025-10-24      18 of  11,238 train rows cross the cut    0.160%
    2026-03-23      29 of  24,382 train rows cross the cut    0.119%
  No embargo is applied. At this magnitude it cannot move a quantile. For a
  CONDITIONAL model the same shortcut would not be safe -- the licence here
  is the count, not the model class.

  The HALVES ANALYSIS numbers, QUOTED from NUMBERS.md and not recomputed:
    cut 2026-07-18   near half 1.06pp   far half 3.05pp   effect 1.99pp
    MAE over-covers, MFE under-covers, both halves, magnitude grows
    by horizon: near half 0.86 -> 1.29pp, far half 1.35 -> 4.33pp (0.5h to 10h)

